model
  schema 1.1

# Authorization Model v2 — Kachō (rbac-2026 Contract-A: FLAT INDEX).
#
# Написана на DSL отношений (наследие внешнего движка, который её когда-то
# исполнял). Движок снят стадией S6 эпика #747; ЯЗЫК остался, и разбирает его
# теперь сама служба — `services/iam/internal/authzmodel` компилирует отсюда план
# вывода, которым реляционная форма считает вердикт. Прошедшее время в разборах
# ниже относится к тому движку и оставлено как история, а не как описание
# сегодняшнего тракта.
#
# This file is the **canonical source** of the authorization model, and since the
# external relations engine was retired (S6 of epic #747) it is the source the
# DECISION itself compiles from: `services/iam/internal/authzmodel` embeds a
# byte-identical copy and builds the resolution plan the relational form answers
# with. The two copies are kept identical by `make -C deploy fga-model-embed`;
# edit the canonical file only.
#
# The engine's model-stub configmap and the target that produced its JSON are
# gone with the engine: nothing converts this file to the engine's wire format
# any more, because there is no engine to feed.
#
# rbac-2026 Contract-A (Explicit RBAC model 2026, acceptance §0 D-5 / §9):
# for TENANT roles the model is a **FLAT INDEX** (Zanzibar-as-storage). There is
# no `scope_grant` carrier (`type scope_grant` + `sg_*` pointers + `g_*_<type>`
# grant-resolvers, #177 — all removed), and tenant access is materialized
# PER-OBJECT by the iam reconciler; Check is a direct tuple lookup.
#
# THE THREE TOP LEVELS ARE THE EXCEPTION, AND THEY CASCADE (owner directive
# 2026-07-27, .claude/rules/security.md "Три уровня супер-доступа — КАСКАДОМ"):
#
#   1. cloud administrator  — `cluster:cluster_kacho_root#system_admin`, over everything;
#   2. bootstrap identity   — the SAME relation (migration 0058 seeds it for the
#                             bootstrap SA, seed/bootstrap_admin.go for the root
#                             user), so it needs no separate carrier;
#   3. account administrator — `account:<id>#admin` (which derives `or owner`),
#                             WITHIN its own account only.
#
# Why these three cascade while everything below stays flat: materializing them
# breaks the exact scenario they exist for. With a lagging or broken drainer the
# person who has to repair the platform would himself be locked out, precisely
# when he is needed. A cascade resolves at request time and works regardless of
# the state of the materialization pipeline. There are also only a handful of
# such principals and their rights are never revoked selectively, so paying for
# per-object materialization buys nothing. Incident it fixes (2026-07-26): every
# verb on `iam_access_binding` was a direct userset with no `or`, so a cluster
# administrator saw every grant but could revoke only his own — 652 denials
# against 32 allows in one run; an employee left and his colleague's access could
# not be revoked except by editing the database by hand.
#
# The cascade is carried by ONE computed relation per verb-bearing type:
#
#	define super_admin: <derivation over this type's OWN structural pointer>
#
# unioned into every verb the type declares and into its `admin` tier (the other
# tiers derive from `admin`, so they follow). `project.super_admin` is derived
# from its ACCOUNT
# and the CLUSTER and deliberately NOT from `project.admin` — project scope and
# below stay flat, which keeps the anti-over-grant boundary of data-integrity.md
# intact. Behavioural lock: authzmap/super_admin_cascade_test.go.
#
# THE ACCOUNT OWNER IS A STRUCTURAL SOURCE ON HIS OWN ACCOUNT OBJECT (owner
# refinement 2026-07-27, same rules section). An account is created by
# self-service, so tearing it down must be as reliable as creating it — and it was
# not: the right to delete arrived with the owner ROLE BINDING, materialized per
# object by the reconciler, so a just-created account could not be deleted until
# the queue caught up, while the cloud administrator's equivalent right cascaded
# and always worked. `account`'s verbs therefore read `or owner` directly.
# NOT via the `admin` tier: `account.admin` also accepts DIRECT subjects, so
# reading it would hand the DELEGATED account administrator the account object
# itself — and the recorded picture puts him inside the account, not over it
# (levels: cloud → everything; owner → structurally his own account; account
# administrator → cascade WITHIN the account, never onto it; below → flat, by
# grant). Locks: authzmap/account_owner_structural_test.go.
#
# Deliberately NOT cascaded (they are not CRUD authority): `owner` and `subject`
# (identity facts — no tier ever CONFERS ownership; the refinement above reads
# `owner` as a source of verbs, it never grants the relation itself),
# `member` (membership), `announce_writer` (data plane only),
# `fga_writer` (proxy), `use` / `token_creator` / `ssh` / `console` (these derive
# from `admin`/`editor` on their own types and so follow the tier, by their
# pre-existing definition — not by anything added here).
#
# What is KEPT and why:
#   - direct relation-tuples on every type (`v_*`, tier viewer/editor/admin) —
#     the materialized flat index for tenant roles;
#   - cluster super-admin relations (system_admin/system_viewer/...) — both the
#     D-9 Go short-circuit anchor and, now, the root of the cascade above;
#   - STRUCTURAL parent-pointer relations (`account: [account]`,
#     `project: [project]`, `cluster: [cluster]`, `parent: [...]`) — needed for
#     list-by-account, owner-binding lifecycle and the cluster-singleton
#     write-enforcement. Only `super_admin` derives over them; no tenant access
#     cascade reads them. Each one on a consumer-owned type must have a producer
#     in the tree or an explicit `# kacho:latent` marker;
#   - CEL conditions (unchanged).
#
# `v_create` IS NOT PART OF THE OBJECT VERB-SET, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT.
# A verb relation names an operation performed ON the object the tuple points at.
# "Create" is not one: at the moment authority is decided the object does not yet
# exist, so the question is always asked of a PARENT. Kachō answers it with the
# write tier on that parent — `editor@project` gates every resource Create in the
# permission catalog — which is why a per-object `v_create` had no reader anywhere.
# It was nonetheless declared by 24 types and materialized on every one of them:
# 41087 tuples on the reference stand, 9.05% of the whole store, all of them
# answering a question nothing asks. Worse than the cost, a relation that is
# granted, written and never checked is a right nobody reviews, and the role editor
# offered `create` on 23 resources where authoring it delivered exactly nothing.
#
# ONE type keeps it, on container semantics: `registry_registry`. "Create a
# repository in this namespace" IS an operation on the registry object, and it is
# really asked — control plane (CreateRepository / RenameRepository) and data plane
# (docker push to a new repo, cross-repo mount, first-push blob-reveal guard).
#
# Both directions are locked by authzmap/verb_relation_has_reader_test.go: a
# reader-less carrier reappearing reds with the type name, and so does an exemption
# that outlived its subject.
#
# Contract-B (acceptance D-12) REMOVED the B2B `type organization` and every
# `... from organization` derivation — Organization is fully gone.
#
# DSL conventions (acceptance §4):
#   - `cluster:cluster_kacho_root`     — singleton enforced on write-side
#     (P3-D17). One global cluster object.
#   - `account` / `project` — STRUCTURAL parents
#     (cluster ▶ account ▶ project ▶ <domain resource>);
#     parent-pointers only, no access cascade (Contract-A flat).
#   - per-domain object types — `vpc_network`, `vpc_subnet`, `vpc_address`,
#     `vpc_security_group`, `vpc_route_table`, `vpc_gateway`,
#     `vpc_network_interface`, `vpc_address_pool`, `vpc_cidr_group`,
#     `compute_instance`, `compute_guest_access_key`, `compute_placement_group`,
#     `storage_volume`, `storage_snapshot`, `storage_image`,
#     `nlb_network_load_balancer`, `nlb_target_group`,
#     `nlb_listener` (KAC-178 §4 — Listener as first-class FGA object),
#     `registry_registry`, `registry_repository`,
#     `iam_role`, `iam_access_binding`, `iam_service_account`, `iam_group`,
#     `iam_user`.
#   - relations: `viewer` / `editor` / `admin` / `owner` / `member` per the
#     Kachō roles vocabulary (KAC-121).
#   - conditional relations: `with` clause names a Condition (built-in or
#     custom) — FGA pre-compiles CEL on `WriteAuthorizationModel`.
#
# The per-domain enumeration above is DERIVED from the type declarations below,
# not a hand-kept summary, and it is pinned in BOTH directions by
# authzmap/fga_model_header_census_test.go. That bullet must stay a bare list:
# the gate reads every backticked identifier in it, so prose belongs here, in a
# paragraph of its own. Before the gate existed the list had drifted both ways at
# once — it named a type retired long ago (its tombstone is further down) and
# omitted an entire domain the body declares. A list nobody verifies drifts from
# its subject; this one now expires with it.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Generic principal / subject types.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

type user

type service_account

# group — subject-grouping type. `group#member` usersets are accepted as
# direct subjects on hierarchy + resource relations.
#
# MEMBERSHIP IS ONE LEVEL DEEP, and the model says so because the schema does:
# `group_members_type_check` (0001_initial.sql) admits `user` and
# `service_account` only. A group is never a member of a group, and no external
# principal type exists — there is no producer for one anywhere in the tree.
# Listing either here would promise a capability that no input can reach: the
# insert rejects it before any row is written. Decision and both outcomes:
# services/iam/docs/engineering/architecture/group-membership-is-one-level.md
# (kacho#734). The two declarations are held together by the gate
# TestGroupMemberKindsAgreeBetweenModelAndSchema.
type group
  relations
    define member: [user, service_account]

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cluster — singleton "cluster_kacho_root". Write-side enforcement rejects
# tuples with any other object id (P3-D17).
#   - system_admin  — standing cluster administrator.
#   - system_viewer — read-only cluster catalog viewer.
#   - billing_admin — cluster-wide billing.
#   - console       — cluster console access, MFA-gated.
#   - any_admin     — alias for system_admin (kept as a relation name so
#                     hierarchy `any_admin from cluster` references stay
#                     valid across many downstream type definitions).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

type cluster
  relations
    define system_admin: [user, service_account]
    define system_viewer: [user, service_account]
    define billing_admin: [user, service_account]
    define console: [user with mfa_fresh, service_account with mfa_fresh]
    define any_admin: system_admin
    # KAC-178 §3: viewer — общий read-relation для глобальных reference-данных
    # (compute.Region, compute.Zone, …), к которым каталог permissions
    # привязан через scope_extractor {object_type: cluster, from_request_field: '*'}.
    # `user:*` userset делает справочные данные читаемыми ЛЮБЫМ authenticated
    # subject'ом (как и должно быть для tenant-facing reference-data). Также
    # включает system_viewer / any_admin transitively. До этого relation'а на
    # cluster не было `viewer` вообще → catalog `required_relation: "viewer"`
    # на cluster-scope'ed RPC всегда возвращал deny → UI dashboard падал.
    define viewer: [user, user:*, service_account] or system_viewer or any_admin
    # KAC-198 follow-up: editor + admin — derived-relation aliases.
    # iam/internal/service/authorize_service.go::resolveActionToRelation
    # maps verbs to canonical relations:
    #   get / list      → viewer    (covered above)
    #   create / update → editor
    #   delete          → admin
    # Cluster-scoped Internal admin RPCs (compute.regions.delete, etc.)
    # land on object cluster:cluster_kacho_root with derived relation
    # admin/editor. Without these aliases, FGA Check returned no-path for
    # every system_admin user even though their tuple was correct.
    # `system_admin` covers BOTH editor + admin so a single tuple grants
    # full mutation rights. `any_admin` is an alias for system_admin.
    define editor: [user, service_account] or system_admin or any_admin
    define admin: [user, service_account] or system_admin or any_admin
    # quota_reader — узкое право читать ДЕЙСТВУЮЩИЕ потолки арендатора и их
    # дельту (InternalLimitService.Resolve / ListChangedSince, issue #291).
    # Выдаётся служебным учёткам ВЛАДЕЛЬЦЕВ считаемых типов ресурсов; человеку
    # оно не нужно — администратор облака читает пределы CRUD-глаголами, и
    # `system_admin` покрывает это отношение ниже.
    #
    # Почему отдельное отношение, а не `viewer` и не `system_viewer`. `viewer` на
    # кластере НАМЕРЕННО выполняется подстановкой (`user:*` выше) — это
    # глобальный справочник размещения, который обязан читать каждый
    # аутентифицированный арендатор; проверка против него отвечала бы «да» всем и
    # выглядела бы при этом полноценным гейтом. `system_viewer` — вся
    # кластерная поверхность чтения: выдать её ради двух чисел значит выдать
    # много больше самой способности. Здесь — ровно эта способность и ничего
    # сверх неё.
    #
    # Почему `group#member`, а не перечисление учёток. Получателей будет больше
    # одного и состав будет меняться: сегодня считает потолки владелец сети,
    # следом — владельцы хранилища, балансировщика, реестра и машин. Снять одного
    # из группы — одна строка членства; снять одного из перечисления — снятие
    # кортежей по всем объектам (data-integrity.md B18).
    define quota_reader: [service_account, group#member] or system_admin

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Identity & hierarchy — account → project. (B2B Organization fully removed in
# RBAC-2026 Contract-B; cluster is the only structural parent above account.)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

type account
  relations
    define cluster: [cluster]
    # Levels 1-2 (cloud administrator / bootstrap identity, both carried by
    # cluster#system_admin) reach the account over its structural cluster pointer.
    # The account's OWN admin is deliberately absent: level 3 cascades WITHIN the
    # account, and the account object is the boundary of that scope, not inside it.
    define super_admin: any_admin from cluster
    define owner: [user]
    define admin: [user, service_account, group#member] or owner or super_admin
    define editor: [user, service_account, group#member] or admin
    # Contract-A (flat): the SEC-L `or system_viewer from cluster` read-cascade is
    # REMOVED — account.viewer is no longer derived from a cluster relation.
    # Visibility is materialized per-object by the iam reconciler (direct viewer
    # tuples on each account).
    #
    # This note used to name the network operator as the consumer of that
    # materialization, in the present tense. It outlived its subject: the
    # component never existed in the tree, its service account and grants were
    # retired by migration 0081, and its trusted-forwarder pins were removed the
    # same day. A comment that keeps naming a retired consumer sends the next
    # reader looking for a caller that cannot be found — and the model is the one
    # place where that search costs the most.
    # Contract-B: the `... from organization` disjuncts and the `organization`
    # parent-pointer are REMOVED — B2B Organization is fully gone (D-12).
    define viewer: [user, service_account, group#member] or editor
    define billing_admin: [user, service_account] or admin
    # rbac-2026 P2 (D-6): account is a verb-bearing resource. These v_* relations
    # are object-level read/write on the account OBJECT ITSELF; a grant of e.g.
    # `iam.account.get` materializes `account:<id> # v_get @ subj` → "see the
    # account in a selector without any access to its contents" (D-2: no implicit
    # hierarchy cascade into content). Object verb-set, in parity with leaf types
    # (see the `v_create` note in the header: creating an account is not an
    # operation ON an account, so no `v_create` here).
    #
    # Exactly two derivations, and no others:
    #   `owner`       — the account is created by SELF-SERVICE, so deleting it must
    #                   be as reliable as creating it. Structural, so it holds the
    #                   instant the account exists and never waits for the
    #                   reconciler to materialize the owner binding (2026-07-27).
    #   `super_admin` — levels 1-2 (cloud administrator / bootstrap identity).
    # NOT the `admin` tier: it accepts DIRECT subjects, so reading it would give the
    # DELEGATED account administrator the account object itself — he governs what is
    # INSIDE the account, and the account is the boundary of that scope, not a thing
    # within it. Structural lock: authzmap/account_owner_structural_test.go.
    define v_get: [user, service_account, group#member] or owner or super_admin
    define v_list: [user, service_account, group#member] or owner or super_admin
    define v_update: [user, service_account, group#member] or owner or super_admin
    define v_delete: [user, service_account, group#member] or owner or super_admin

type project
  relations
    define account: [account]
    # Contract-A (flat): `cluster` is kept ONLY as a structural parent-pointer
    # (project:<id>#cluster@cluster tuple written on Create) — the SEC-L
    # `system_viewer from cluster` read-cascade is removed (per-object now).
    define cluster: [cluster]
    # Levels 1-3. `admin from account` is the level-3 disjunct and already contains
    # levels 1-2 (account.admin derives super_admin); `any_admin from cluster` is the
    # direct path over the project's own cluster pointer. The project's OWN admin is
    # NOT a source — project scope and below stay flat (anti-over-grant, D-2).
    define super_admin: admin from account or any_admin from cluster
    define admin: [user, service_account, group#member] or super_admin
    define editor: [user, service_account, group#member] or admin
    define viewer: [user, service_account, group#member] or editor
    # rbac-2026 P2 (D-6): project becomes a verb-bearing resource. Same contract
    # as account.v_* — DIRECT subjects only, NO cascade, NOT implied by tiers.
    # A grant materializes `project:<id> # v_get @ subj` (object-level access to
    # the project itself, never to its contents — D-2). Object verb-set, in parity
    # with leaf resource types. Creating resources IN a project is authorized by
    # the write tier on the project (`editor@project`), not by a per-object
    # `v_create` — see the `v_create` note in the header.
    define v_get: [user, service_account, group#member] or super_admin
    define v_list: [user, service_account, group#member] or super_admin
    define v_update: [user, service_account, group#member] or super_admin
    define v_delete: [user, service_account, group#member] or super_admin

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# IAM resources. Group / ServiceAccount / Role / User are account-scoped
# (their `parent` relation cascades from the owning Account); AccessBinding
# / Condition are project-scoped (their `parent` cascades from the Project
# the binding is attached to).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

type iam_user
  relations
    define account: [account]
    define super_admin: admin from account
    define subject: [user]
    define admin: [user, service_account, group#member] or super_admin
    # editor / viewer — directly-assignable (subject-list), а не computed-only:
    # reconciler пишет back-compat tier-tuple (`iam_user:<id> # viewer @ subj`),
    # и computed-only relation (`viewer: subject or editor`) отверг бы такую запись
    # (poison). v_* НЕ union-ятся в tier (Design B развязка, D-6a).
    define editor: [user, service_account, group#member] or admin
    define viewer: [user, service_account, group#member] or subject or editor
    # `subject` — сам пользователь. Он ЧИТАЕТ свою запись, и это не послабление,
    # а восстановление того, что уже было задумано и уже эмитируется: кортеж
    # `iam_user:<usr> # subject @ user:<usr>` пишется на заведении пользователя
    # (services/iam/.../user/internal_upsert.go), и его собственный комментарий
    # говорит, что он существует «чтобы пользователь мог получить сам себя».
    #
    # Но читающий глагол — `v_get`, а `subject` стоял только в `viewer`. Разводка
    # тиров и глаголов (Design B) оставила кортеж без потребителя: он писался,
    # проверкой не читался, и самочтение не работало НИ У КОГО — ни у обычного
    # арендатора, ни у администратора своего же аккаунта. Со стороны это выглядит
    # как «пользователя нет» (скрытие существования отвечает тем же текстом, что
    # и настоящее отсутствие), поэтому дефект был тихим.
    #
    # Добавляется ТОЛЬКО к чтению. Править и удалять себя из факта «я — это я» не
    # следует: это другие вопросы, и у них другие источники права.
    define v_get: [user, service_account, group#member] or subject or super_admin
    define v_list: [user, service_account, group#member] or super_admin
    define v_update: [user, service_account, group#member] or super_admin
    define v_delete: [user, service_account, group#member] or super_admin

type iam_service_account
  relations
    define account: [account]
    define super_admin: admin from account
    define admin: [user, service_account, group#member] or super_admin
    define editor: [user, service_account, group#member] or admin
    define viewer: [user, service_account, group#member] or editor
    define user: [user, service_account] or editor
    define token_creator: [user, service_account] or admin
    define v_get: [user, service_account, group#member] or super_admin
    define v_list: [user, service_account, group#member] or super_admin
    define v_update: [user, service_account, group#member] or super_admin
    define v_delete: [user, service_account, group#member] or super_admin

type iam_group
  relations
    define account: [account]
    define super_admin: admin from account
    define member: [user, service_account]
    define admin: [user, service_account, group#member] or super_admin
    define editor: [user, service_account, group#member] or admin
    define viewer: [user, service_account, group#member] or editor
    define v_get: [user, service_account, group#member] or super_admin
    define v_list: [user, service_account, group#member] or super_admin
    define v_update: [user, service_account, group#member] or super_admin
    define v_delete: [user, service_account, group#member] or super_admin

type iam_role
  relations
    define account: [account]
    define super_admin: admin from account
    define admin: [user, service_account, group#member] or super_admin
    # editor / viewer — directly-assignable (poison-fix): reconciler пишет
    # back-compat tier-tuple, computed-only relation отверг бы запись. v_* НЕ
    # union-ятся в tier (Design B развязка, D-6a).
    define editor: [user, service_account, group#member] or admin
    define viewer: [user, service_account, group#member] or editor
    define v_get: [user, service_account, group#member] or super_admin
    define v_list: [user, service_account, group#member] or super_admin
    define v_update: [user, service_account, group#member] or super_admin
    define v_delete: [user, service_account, group#member] or super_admin

type iam_access_binding
  relations
    define project: [project]
    define account: [account]
    define cluster: [cluster]
    # A binding carries exactly ONE parent-pointer, named after its own scope
    # (access_binding/tuples.go::hierarchyParentTuple), so all three are needed:
    # project-scoped bindings resolve over `project`, account-scoped over `account`,
    # cluster-scoped over `cluster`. This is the type the 2026-07-26 incident hit.
    define super_admin: super_admin from project or admin from account or any_admin from cluster
    define admin: [user, service_account, group#member] or super_admin
    define editor: [user, service_account, group#member] or admin
    define viewer: [user, service_account, group#member] or editor
    define v_get: [user, service_account, group#member] or super_admin
    define v_list: [user, service_account, group#member] or super_admin
    define v_update: [user, service_account, group#member] or super_admin
    define v_delete: [user, service_account, group#member] or super_admin

# TOMBSTONE — `type iam_condition` жил здесь ради тенантского ресурса условия.
# Ресурс снят целиком (сервис, хранилище, наложение на привязку), поэтому у типа
# не осталось ни одного производителя кортежей. Порядок снятия — сначала
# производитель, потом тип: обратный порядок превратил бы каждую его запись в
# отказ, который дренаж считает временным, и заклинил бы голову партиции.
#
# Условия на кортеже (`condition …` в конце этого файла и ограничения
# `… with mfa_fresh`) к снятому типу отношения не имеют и остаются живыми.

# SEC-A/SEC-C — FGA-proxy authority object. Modules (kacho-vpc / kacho-compute /
# kacho-nlb) NEVER write to FGA directly; they call InternalIAMService.
# RegisterResource/UnregisterResource, gated by a ReBAC Check
# `service_account:<sva>#fga_writer@iam_fgaproxy:system` (authzguard.FGAProxyGate,
# fgaProxyObject="iam_fgaproxy:system"). Without this type the Check is rejected
# движком ("object type is undefined in the authorization model" → status 400),
# the gate denies every module, and NO owner-hierarchy tuple is ever written — so
# resource creators cannot view their own resources once real authz is enforced.
# The fga_writer tuples are seeded by kacho-iam migration 0009 (least-priv).
type iam_fgaproxy
  relations
    define fga_writer: [service_account]

